 # Codex Windows Completion Reminder
 
-A small Windows tray companion for Codex Desktop. It can show a persistent, non-activating popup when a task completes or Codex requests tool approval. The popup stays on top, does not take keyboard focus, and closes on a keyboard key or mouse button while passing that input through.
+A standalone Windows tray companion for Codex Desktop. A completed assistant turn can show a persistent, topmost popup without taking focus. A key or mouse-button press dismisses it and passes through to the current app.
 
-This project targets **Codex Desktop on Windows**. Codex CLI, WSL, remote sessions, and service sessions are not supported in v1.
+## Channels and support
 
-## Features
+- Completion: custom only, native only, both, or off.
+- Approval and conversation choice: custom notifications are currently unavailable. Explicitly choose native or off; there is no automatic native fallback.
+- New settings default to custom-only completion and off for approval/choice. Legacy unsupported custom approval/choice modes normalize to off; explicitly saved native preferences are retained.
+- Per-event wording and shared size/colors support previews and reset. Unsupported-event wording is retained as a sample only.
 
-- Separate notification preferences for task completion, tool approval, and conversation-choice requests.
-- Choose custom popup, Codex native notification, both, or off for each category.
-- Edit a title and description per category; adjust popup width, height, and four colors with a live preview.
-- Pause temporarily, disable this companion persistently, or uninstall it.
-- No network calls. The hook passes only event type and an opaque event/turn ID to the local companion. It does not store or transmit prompts, command text, or key/mouse input.
+Saving channels synchronizes three notification preferences in the user-level `config.toml` `[desktop]` section. Custom-only/off disables the corresponding native notification; native/both enables it. This affects Codex globally, not just one chat. Restart Codex to reload native preferences. Windows notification permission, native wording, and duration remain controlled by the OS/app.
 
-## Event support
+Verified against Desktop `26.1007.2314.0`: `notifications-turn-mode` accepts `off/unfocused/always`; `notifications-permissions-enabled` and `notifications-questions-enabled` are booleans. This companion uses `always` when enabling native completion. [Official notifications guide](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/notifications)
 
-- **Task completed:** Codex `Stop` hook.
-- **Tool approval:** Codex `PermissionRequest` hook. The hook never approves or denies the request; Codex's normal approval prompt and policy remain in control.
-- **Conversation choice/input:** Codex Desktop's native question notification is the supported fallback. The current public Hook API does not expose a stable custom event for every conversation-choice prompt, so selecting a custom popup for this category falls back to the native notification. The app does not guess from message punctuation or text.
+## Events
 
-Defaults for a new settings file are custom popups for task completion and tool approval, and Codex native notifications for conversation-choice requests. An existing settings file keeps the user's saved channel choices.
+Completion uses the `Stop` hook: the assistant turn ended, not necessarily the user's overall task. A turn ending with a question can also trigger it.
 
-`Stop` marks the end of an assistant turn, not proof that the user's overall task is finished. If the assistant ends a turn by asking a question, the custom completion popup may appear alongside Codex's native question notification. The default remains the custom completion popup; choose native or off for completion if you prefer to avoid that pairing.
+`PermissionRequest` is retained for diagnosis only and never returns an approval decision. It can fire before automatic review, so it does not reliably mean a human must act. It no longer generates a custom popup.
 
-Codex native notifications are managed by Codex and Windows. This app does not change those settings. Enable the relevant notification category in Codex Settings and allow Windows notifications for the ChatGPT/Codex app. Native notification wording and duration remain controlled by Codex/Windows. See the [Codex notifications guide](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/notifications).
+The protocol has structured waiting states, but a reliable external subscription to the running desktop instance has not been validated. Custom approval/choice notification is unavailable. The app does not guess from punctuation or reply text. Queue/priority machinery is retained for a future validated event source.
 
-## Build
+v1 targets Windows Codex Desktop only. CLI, WSL, remote/service sessions, and cloud-orchestrated local hooks are not promised.
 
-Requirements: Windows with .NET Framework 4.x and Windows PowerShell.
+## Build and install
 
-```powershell
-.\build.ps1
-```
-
-The build compiles `CodexReminder.exe` and `CodexReminderHook.exe` into `dist/` and runs the built-in settings/route self-tests.
-
-## Install and update
-
-For a prebuilt install, download the Windows ZIP from GitHub Releases, extract it, and run `install.ps1` in PowerShell. The included binaries are unsigned; see the release trust note below.
-
-To build from source, clone the repository and run `build.ps1` first, then `install.ps1`:
+Requires Windows, .NET Framework 4.x, and Windows PowerShell. From source:
 
 ```powershell
+.\build.ps1
 .\install.ps1
 ```
 
-The installer builds the app, copies it to `%USERPROFILE%\.codex\codex-reminder`, merges only this app's `Stop` and `PermissionRequest` handlers into `%USERPROFILE%\.codex\hooks.json`, and adds a per-user Startup entry. Existing hooks and Codex notification settings are preserved.
-
-Restart Codex and review/trust the updated hook definitions when prompted. Hooks that have not been reviewed and trusted are skipped by Codex.
+For prebuilt installation, extract the Release ZIP and run `install.ps1` from your own Windows PowerShell. Installation copies binaries to `%USERPROFILE%\.codex\codex-reminder`, merges owned Stop/PermissionRequest hooks, and creates a user Startup entry. Installation itself does not change native notification preferences; saving channels does.
 
-## Settings
+Restart Codex and review/trust changed hook definitions. Start the tray app from File Explorer, not a Codex isolated terminal: the latter may place it on an invisible `CodexSandboxDesktop`. The hook avoids auto-starting it on that isolated desktop.
 
-Right-click the tray icon and choose **提醒设置…**. Settings are saved per Windows user under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\CodexCompletionReminder\settings.json`.
+## Settings and configuration safety
 
-- The global switch and per-event channel choices control this companion's custom popups. The native Codex channel is still controlled by Codex/Windows settings.
-- For the conversation-choice category, the settings window explains that custom event detection is unavailable and native notifications are used as fallback.
-- The temporary pause only applies until the app exits. The global switch persists across restarts.
-- Choosing **退出提醒器** closes the current process; it does not uninstall the hook or disable a future launch. Use the global switch to disable reminders or run the uninstaller to remove the integration.
+Right-click the tray icon and choose **提醒设置…**. Preferences are stored in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\CodexCompletionReminder\settings.json`.
 
-## Troubleshooting missing reminders
+Only the three notification keys are changed. Other fields, comments, and line endings are preserved. The previous config is backed up to `config.toml.codex-reminder-notifications.bak`. Duplicate or unsupported target syntax is rejected. If JSON persistence fails, the companion rolls back its native-config update.
 
-The installer uses an explicit PowerShell invocation and the JSON `commandWindows` override. A bare quoted executable path evaluates to a string in PowerShell instead of running the program. Review and trust the changed hook definitions in Codex after updating their commands.
+The global enable switch controls custom popups only. Pause lasts until exit. Exiting does not uninstall hooks; a later completion can start the helper again. Windows notification permissions must still be granted separately for native notifications.
 
-Start the tray app from Windows File Explorer. Launching it from a Codex isolated terminal may place its windows on `CodexSandboxDesktop`, invisible on the user's interactive desktop. The hook does not auto-start the app on that isolated desktop.
+## Diagnostics and tests
 
-`%LOCALAPPDATA%\CodexCompletionReminder\hook-status.log` records only event type and transport status. It contains no conversation, command, keyboard, or mouse content. `received-Stop` means the hook ran; `pipe-written` means the message was written, not that a user saw the popup. `pipe-error-UnauthorizedAccessException` indicates a permission boundary blocked transport. Normal hook completion and approval contracts remain unchanged.
+`hook-status.log` next to settings contains only startup, event type, and transport status. No prompts, commands, or keyboard/mouse input are logged. This companion makes no network calls; local IPC carries only event kind and opaque ID.
 
-Use `CodexReminderHook.exe --diagnostic` with a test event in the user's own PowerShell to get a nonzero exit code on transport failure. Tests in Codex's isolated tools can be denied access. A zero hook exit code or a window's visible flag is not an end-to-end desktop acceptance test.
-
-## Uninstall
-
-```powershell
-.\uninstall.ps1
-```
-
-Uninstall removes only this app's hook handlers and Startup entry. It keeps user settings by default. To also remove the settings file:
-
-```powershell
-.\uninstall.ps1 -RemoveSettings
-```
+`received-Stop` confirms hook invocation; `pipe-written` confirms writing, not visual delivery. Isolated-terminal tests can be denied access. Zero exit codes and visible window flags are not desktop acceptance evidence. Feed a test event to `CodexReminderHook.exe --diagnostic` for a nonzero status on transport failure.
 
-## Tests
+Installed hook commands explicitly invoke PowerShell and use JSON `commandWindows`, avoiding bare quoted paths that only print a string.
 
 ```powershell
 .\tests\Run-Tests.ps1
 ```
 
-The automated tests cover defaults, config serialization and normalization, channel routing, hook output behavior, and safe install/uninstall merging. The release checklist also requires a manual desktop test for foreground/background popups, key/mouse dismissal, click-through, Codex native notification fallback, and multi-monitor sizing.
+Tests cover settings, routing, native-config preservation/rollback, hook output contracts, shell execution with spaces in paths, and safe install/uninstall. Clean-Windows, foreground/background, input pass-through, multiple displays, and DPI still require manual acceptance.
 
-## Release trust
+## Uninstall and release
 
-GitHub Release executables are unsigned. Verify the SHA-256 file attached to a release, or build from source. Windows may show a SmartScreen warning for unsigned binaries.
+Run `uninstall.ps1` to remove owned hooks, Startup entry, and binaries while preserving unrelated hooks. User settings and chosen native notification states remain. To restore native alerts, select native and save before uninstalling, or re-enable them in Codex Settings. Do not overwrite later config changes with an entire old backup.
 
-## License
+`uninstall.ps1 -RemoveSettings` also removes companion settings and diagnostic logs.
 
-MIT. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).
+Release executables are unsigned. Verify SHA-256 or build from source. MIT; see [LICENSE](LICENSE).
